+    {
+      "id": "L-k01-all-stone-house-2788",
+      "title": "The K01 all-stone house: the front's K02 courses laid round every face, its corners, belts, heads and parapets reconstructed",
+      "section": "per_subject",
+      "subjects": [],
+      "covers": [],
+      "recorded": "2026-10-18",
+      "revised": null,
+      "fields": [
+        {
+          "label": "Decision",
+          "text": "a K01 record may name `construction: \"stone\"` (T-2788): its K02 `stone_front` — fabric, dressing and coursing — is laid round the whole envelope, so a Bedford-stone house is not a stone front on a brick box. `generators/archetypes/k01_frontage.py` lays both flanks, the rear and every masonry rear wing's walls through the street front's own coursing (`Assembly.stone_front`), struck on the front's bed lines up to each face's wall top, so every course runs level round every corner. Each face carries the front's rusticated base and coping and its belts, run round in place of the brick house's K03 string course; the K06 sills are seated on every face and every opening off the street front takes a K02 flat arch of voussoirs as its head, where a brick wall takes a brick segmental or soldier head; a kit seated on a face (a sill, a bay, a stoop) has its stones dressed smooth and flush under it, as on the front. A flat roof's side parapets are in the house's stone, a timber bay's plinth is the house's stone carried out under it, and a stone bay wears the house's own K02 fabric. A timber (`frame`) wing stays timber. The K15 stacks keep whatever fabric the record's stack names. **What is invented.** That the flanks and rear are laid as the front is — the same course heights, block lengths, joints and dressing, the same base and coping; a real house often dressed its street front more richly than its sides. That the corners are the two faces' courses meeting (no quoin, which is stone returned into brick, so an all-stone record names no `corner_bond`); on a rock-faced wall the two faces' rock stands proud of each corner without wrapping it. That the belts are the front's, at the front's floor lines, on every face. That every opening off the front has a flat arch rather than a lintel. **What is not.** Nothing here is attested for any house; no structure record names it yet. 1801 Prairie (T-2789) is the first that will, on the Taylor photograph and the 1888 Inland Architect plate's reading of its stone."
+        },
+        {
+          "label": "How to resolve",
+          "text": "a house's own photographs, drawings or descriptions of its side and rear walls give their coursing, corners, belts and heads, and that house takes them in place of the front's."
+        },
+        {
+          "label": "Ticket",
+          "text": "T-2788 (piece 2 of T-2539)."
+        },
+        {
+          "label": "Review",
+          "text": "`tools/check_stone_house_kit.py --check` builds four K01 houses as all-stone and measures it."
+        },
+        {
+          "label": "Recorded",
+          "text": "2026-10-18 (T-2788)."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
